WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.772
This is AI Playbook for Schools:

00:00:02.812 --> 00:00:06.326
a responsible AI guide that helps school staff choose tools

00:00:06.366 --> 00:00:09.330
that protect student privacy and work for every student.

00:00:10.332 --> 00:00:12.814
School staff are the experts on their students,

00:00:12.854 --> 00:00:15.707
and AI is already part of their work:

00:00:15.747 --> 00:00:18.078
about six in ten teachers in the U.S.,

00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:20.123
and two in three teachers in Canada,

00:00:20.163 --> 00:00:23.316
use AI tools. Here's the problem.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:26.408
Every week brings a new AI tool to judge:

00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:28.194
what it costs, whether it's fair,

00:00:28.234 --> 00:00:30.652
and whether student data stays safe.

00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:33.554
Each school has to answer those questions on its own,

00:00:33.594 --> 00:00:35.982
often with only the vendor's marketing to go on.

00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:40.004
Elizabeth Larkin is an AI-native developer

00:00:40.044 --> 00:00:42.175
and analyst with Stone Arch Collective,

00:00:42.215 --> 00:00:45.097
a Minnesota cooperative of AI practitioners who

00:00:45.137 --> 00:00:47.751
help schools and organizations use AI well.

00:00:47.791 --> 00:00:49.705
While she was helping high school leaders in a

00:00:49.745 --> 00:00:52.883
Minnesota public school district plan how to use AI,

00:00:52.923 --> 00:00:56.090
they told her that they and their staff spend a lot of

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.840
time checking AI tools against their district's AI policy.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.465
So we built a playbook that starts with their role,

00:01:02.505 --> 00:01:04.340
and puts the facts side by side,

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.057
so they can choose tools faster

00:01:06.097 --> 00:01:07.793
and spend that time on students.

00:01:08.795 --> 00:01:11.944
Special educators know what their students need.

00:01:11.984 --> 00:01:13.601
Pick your role, and the tools built

00:01:13.641 --> 00:01:15.195
for that work move to the front.

00:01:16.197 --> 00:01:19.471
Filter by cost and by the systems your district already runs,

00:01:19.511 --> 00:01:21.804
so nobody starts from scratch.

00:01:21.844 --> 00:01:24.677
Not sure how a filter works? Tap the i.

00:01:25.679 --> 00:01:28.460
Every badge is a fact from the company's own website,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.831
explained in plain words. Tap it

00:01:30.871 --> 00:01:34.229
to see exactly what they promise, one topic at a time.

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:37.820
The scouting report shows where your data goes:

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.717
where it's stored, and where it may be sent to be processed,

00:01:40.757 --> 00:01:42.441
even when the company doesn't say.

00:01:42.481 --> 00:01:44.817
It also lists what a tool doesn't offer,

00:01:44.857 --> 00:01:46.789
so staff can ask the right questions

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:48.869
before any student information goes in.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:51.822
If a company hasn't published a fact,

00:01:51.862 --> 00:01:55.078
we don't guess. It's listed under Under review,

00:01:55.118 --> 00:01:56.860
and we recheck every tool together.

00:01:57.913 --> 00:02:01.468
Copy-ready prompts protect student privacy by design.

00:02:01.508 --> 00:02:03.857
And as staff rate tools, the ones people in

00:02:03.897 --> 00:02:05.913
your role trust most rise for your role.

00:02:07.856 --> 00:02:10.694
Behind the scenes, AI does the heavy lifting,

00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:13.931
and people stay in charge. Claude Code helps us

00:02:13.971 --> 00:02:16.550
research and recheck each company's own pages,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.211
and a person approves every fact.

00:02:18.251 --> 00:02:20.355
Voice reviews become text through the

00:02:20.395 --> 00:02:22.418
browser's own speech recognition,

00:02:22.458 --> 00:02:25.056
and a person checks every review before it's shown.

00:02:26.057 --> 00:02:27.997
Anyone can also ask the Coach,

00:02:28.037 --> 00:02:32.163
built with Adaption Labs. Describe your role and your task,

00:02:32.203 --> 00:02:33.925
never student details, and it

00:02:33.965 --> 00:02:35.589
answers from the playbook itself:

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:37.855
the right play, the recommended tools,

00:02:37.895 --> 00:02:40.857
and a reminder to keep student information out of AI.

00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:47.052
Here's the systemic change. Vetting stops being something

00:02:47.092 --> 00:02:49.761
every school repeats alone and becomes a shared,

00:02:49.801 --> 00:02:52.975
public resource: one recheck helps every school,

00:02:53.015 --> 00:02:54.905
including the ones without a tech team.

00:02:54.945 --> 00:02:57.174
Publishing what companies haven't shared

00:02:57.214 --> 00:02:59.590
puts pressure on vendors to share more.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:01.893
And rankings come from staff in each role,

00:03:01.933 --> 00:03:04.002
with fair-average rules that stop gaming,

00:03:04.042 --> 00:03:05.634
and no paid placement, ever.

00:03:06.636 --> 00:03:08.981
Educators decide: they rate,

00:03:09.021 --> 00:03:11.015
review and shape the rankings,

00:03:11.055 --> 00:03:13.455
and every fact links to its source.

00:03:13.495 --> 00:03:15.586
When something's wrong, we fix it in the open.

00:03:16.588 --> 00:03:20.529
Responsible AI isn't only about how a tool is built.

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:22.988
It's also about how it's chosen.

00:03:23.028 --> 00:03:26.724
Every AI tool a teacher picks ends up in front of students:

00:03:26.764 --> 00:03:28.247
their writing, their questions,

00:03:28.287 --> 00:03:30.474
sometimes their data. When school

00:03:30.514 --> 00:03:32.111
staff can see the facts first,

00:03:32.151 --> 00:03:33.786
like where student data goes,

00:03:33.826 --> 00:03:37.097
whether the tool works on every device and in every language,

00:03:37.137 --> 00:03:38.651
and whether it's accessible,

00:03:38.691 --> 00:03:40.305
they choose tools that protect

00:03:40.345 --> 00:03:42.012
students and work for all of them.

00:03:42.052 --> 00:03:44.819
And when every school can make those choices,

00:03:44.859 --> 00:03:48.884
more students get safe, fair access to good learning tools.

00:03:48.924 --> 00:03:52.387
That's what UN Sustainable Development Goal 4 asks for:

00:03:52.427 --> 00:03:55.578
quality education, with equal access for every learner.

00:03:56.579 --> 00:03:58.715
In future phases, members will be able

00:03:58.755 --> 00:04:01.002
to add their district's AI policy,

00:04:01.042 --> 00:04:03.562
so the playbook fits their rules.

00:04:03.602 --> 00:04:06.676
Stone Arch Collective plans to lead a pilot with schools,

00:04:06.716 --> 00:04:09.999
starting in Minnesota. As we learn how AI school

00:04:10.039 --> 00:04:12.501
policies and guidance are applied in other countries,

00:04:12.541 --> 00:04:16.015
including in Canada, we hope to grow beyond the U.S.

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:18.159
We're asking you for introductions to schools

00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:20.029
and educators who want to help shape it.

00:04:22.537 --> 00:04:25.471
AI Playbook for Schools.

00:04:25.511 --> 00:04:27.647
Teach smart. Lead bold. Go team!
